abstract: >-
  A proof of the prime number theorem in its equivalent form M(n)=o(n),
  obtained by comparing successive deletions of largest prime factors from
  uniformly sampled squarefree integers. The limit superior in n of the total
  variation distance between deletion depths K and K+1 is O((log K)^(-1/4)).
  The proof develops the multiplier-comparison method of Richter and
  Bergelson-Richter using a Fourier matching estimate for products of adjacent
  degrees, uniform in the logarithmic width of a moving prime window. It uses
  elementary prime estimates and Plancherel's theorem without assuming the
  prime number theorem. The limit in n is taken first; no rate for M(n)/n or
  new prime-counting error term is obtained. The statement of the deletion
  theorem alone is not claimed as new, and the depth rate is not claimed sharp.
keywords:
  - largest prime factor
  - total variation
  - squarefree integers
  - Moebius function
  - Mertens function
  - prime number theorem
